1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,095
If you audit your bank statement tonight,

2
00:00:03,095 --> 00:00:05,057
you will see every coffee,

3
00:00:05,057 --> 00:00:06,039
subscription,

4
00:00:06,039 --> 00:00:08,681
and utility bill down to the penny.

5
00:00:08,681 --> 00:00:11,775
What you will never see is an invoice for

6
00:00:11,775 --> 00:00:15,323
the skill you postponed learning two years ago,

7
00:00:15,323 --> 00:00:19,022
the side project still sitting in a notes folder,

8
00:00:19,022 --> 00:00:23,400
or the career move you researched until the window closed.

9
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:24,004
In 1914,

10
00:00:24,004 --> 00:00:28,684
Austrian economist Friedrich von Wieser gave this hidden price

11
00:00:28,684 --> 00:00:29,514
tag a name:

12
00:00:29,514 --> 00:00:30,797
opportunity cost,

13
00:00:30,797 --> 00:00:34,270
the value of the best alternative you gave up.

14
00:00:34,270 --> 00:00:36,610
Doing nothing is never neutral.

15
00:00:36,610 --> 00:00:39,705
Every month you say let me wait until the

16
00:00:39,705 --> 00:00:41,063
timing is perfect,

17
00:00:41,063 --> 00:00:44,460
you are paying full price for the status quo.

18
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:45,929
Why do intelligent,

19
00:00:45,929 --> 00:00:48,868
analytical adults fall into this trap?

20
00:00:48,868 --> 00:00:49,487
In 1990,

21
00:00:49,487 --> 00:00:54,513
psychologists Ilana Ritov and Jonathan Baron documented a pattern

22
00:00:54,513 --> 00:00:56,137
called omission bias:

23
00:00:56,137 --> 00:00:59,849
people judge harm caused by acting as worse than

24
00:00:59,849 --> 00:01:02,788
the same harm caused by doing nothing.

25
00:01:02,788 --> 00:01:06,654
If you spend two hundred dollars testing a digital

26
00:01:06,654 --> 00:01:08,742
product and nobody buys it,

27
00:01:08,742 --> 00:01:11,371
the loss is visible and it stings.

28
00:01:11,371 --> 00:01:14,310
If you sit on the idea for four years,

29
00:01:14,310 --> 00:01:16,243
the cost is just as real,

30
00:01:16,243 --> 00:01:17,713
but nobody sees it,

31
00:01:17,713 --> 00:01:20,110
so your brain files it as safe.

32
00:01:20,110 --> 00:01:23,190
In his 2015 letter to Amazon shareholders,

33
00:01:23,190 --> 00:01:26,711
Jeff Bezos described the mental model he used to

34
00:01:26,711 --> 00:01:30,011
keep a giant company from slowing to a crawl.

35
00:01:30,011 --> 00:01:33,532
He divided decisions into Type One and Type Two.

36
00:01:33,532 --> 00:01:36,246
Type One decisions are one-way doors:

37
00:01:36,246 --> 00:01:37,859
once you walk through,

38
00:01:37,859 --> 00:01:39,766
you cannot easily go back,

39
00:01:39,766 --> 00:01:44,093
like taking on massive personal debt or selling a business.

40
00:01:44,093 --> 00:01:46,734
Those deserve weeks of careful math.

41
00:01:46,734 --> 00:01:49,447
Type Two decisions are two-way doors:

42
00:01:49,447 --> 00:01:50,767
you open the door,

43
00:01:50,767 --> 00:01:51,648
look around,

44
00:01:51,648 --> 00:01:53,995
and if you dislike what you see,

45
00:01:53,995 --> 00:01:55,315
you step back out.

46
00:01:55,315 --> 00:01:57,808
Publishing your first video essay,

47
00:01:57,808 --> 00:01:59,495
pitching three clients,

48
00:01:59,495 --> 00:02:04,043
or launching a nineteen-dollar template are all two-way doors.

49
00:02:04,043 --> 00:02:07,710
The trouble starts when people bring Type One fear

50
00:02:07,710 --> 00:02:09,470
to Type Two experiments.

51
00:02:09,470 --> 00:02:13,962
Perfectionists love to call their hesitation thorough research.

52
00:02:13,962 --> 00:02:16,172
In his 2016 shareholder letter,

53
00:02:16,172 --> 00:02:19,808
Jeff Bezos wrote that most decisions should be made

54
00:02:19,808 --> 00:02:24,085
with somewhere around seventy percent of the information you

55
00:02:24,085 --> 00:02:25,012
wish you had.

56
00:02:25,012 --> 00:02:27,222
If you wait for ninety percent,

57
00:02:27,222 --> 00:02:27,864
he wrote,

58
00:02:27,864 --> 00:02:29,860
you are probably being slow.

59
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:32,213
Two things happen while you wait.

60
00:02:32,213 --> 00:02:35,778
Everyone else can see the obvious opportunity too,

61
00:02:35,778 --> 00:02:37,560
so the advantage shrinks.

62
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:42,052
And the missing information rarely lives inside another podcast

63
00:02:42,052 --> 00:02:42,907
or textbook;

64
00:02:42,907 --> 00:02:46,543
it shows up after your work touches the real world.

65
00:02:46,543 --> 00:02:48,682
You cannot steer a parked car,

66
00:02:48,682 --> 00:02:51,320
no matter how long you study the map.

67
00:02:51,320 --> 00:02:56,094
A puzzle named after fourteenth-century philosopher Jean Buridan describes

68
00:02:56,094 --> 00:02:58,803
another trap that haunts analytical minds.

69
00:02:58,803 --> 00:03:02,674
Imagine a hungry donkey standing halfway between two equally

70
00:03:02,674 --> 00:03:03,964
fresh stacks of hay.

71
00:03:03,964 --> 00:03:06,544
Because neither stack is clearly better,

72
00:03:06,544 --> 00:03:10,286
the animal freezes in endless comparison until it starves.

73
00:03:10,286 --> 00:03:13,834
Look at how creators and professionals stall for months

74
00:03:13,834 --> 00:03:16,027
debating Python versus JavaScript,

75
00:03:16,027 --> 00:03:17,511
Notion versus Obsidian,

76
00:03:17,511 --> 00:03:19,705
or YouTube topic A versus topic B.

77
00:03:19,705 --> 00:03:22,608
When two reversible paths look roughly equal,

78
00:03:22,608 --> 00:03:26,220
the difference between them is usually small compared to

79
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:27,962
the cost of standing still.

80
00:03:27,962 --> 00:03:31,510
Pick one before dinner and let execution break the tie.

81
00:03:31,510 --> 00:03:35,446
Moving fast never means gambling recklessly with rent money.

82
00:03:35,446 --> 00:03:39,054
Risk scholar Nassim Taleb calls the smart alternative a

83
00:03:39,054 --> 00:03:40,169
Barbell Strategy:

84
00:03:40,169 --> 00:03:43,581
keep ninety percent of your foundation ultra-stable,

85
00:03:43,581 --> 00:03:47,189
such as a reliable day job and cash emergency reserves,

86
00:03:47,189 --> 00:03:50,469
while using the remaining ten percent of your time

87
00:03:50,469 --> 00:03:51,059
for fast,

88
00:03:51,059 --> 00:03:52,568
asymmetric experiments.

89
00:03:52,568 --> 00:03:54,864
When an experiment costs zero debt,

90
00:03:54,864 --> 00:03:56,570
fifty dollars in software,

91
00:03:56,570 --> 00:03:58,866
and five hours on Saturday morning,

92
00:03:58,866 --> 00:04:02,409
your worst-case scenario is a sharper resume skill and

93
00:04:02,409 --> 00:04:03,261
a good story.

94
00:04:03,261 --> 00:04:07,132
Your best-case scenario is a compounding digital asset that

95
00:04:07,132 --> 00:04:09,100
transforms your income margin.

96
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:10,956
Before closing this video,

97
00:04:10,956 --> 00:04:14,811
run one stalled decision through a five-minute filter.

98
00:04:14,811 --> 00:04:15,739
Question one:

99
00:04:15,739 --> 00:04:18,809
is this truly an irreversible one-way door,

100
00:04:18,809 --> 00:04:21,736
or can you undo it in thirty days without

101
00:04:21,736 --> 00:04:22,806
permanent harm?

102
00:04:22,806 --> 00:04:23,735
Question two:

103
00:04:23,735 --> 00:04:26,590
how can you shrink the first test so the

104
00:04:26,590 --> 00:04:30,374
maximum downside is under one hundred dollars and two

105
00:04:30,374 --> 00:04:31,016
weekends?

106
00:04:31,016 --> 00:04:32,087
Question three:

107
00:04:32,087 --> 00:04:35,513
if you repeat the last twelve months of waiting,

108
00:04:35,513 --> 00:04:39,511
what will that hesitation quietly cost your future self?

109
00:04:39,511 --> 00:04:42,724
If the door swings both ways and the downside

110
00:04:42,724 --> 00:04:43,438
is capped,

111
00:04:43,438 --> 00:04:46,650
stop researching and walk through it tonight.
